@*
    OdsCombobox — a searchable single-select with optional inline create
    (Odyssey Design System · components/Combobox).

    The picker OdsContactSelect / OdsCustodianSelect build on, plus the account and
    party pickers. Type to filter, pick to select; provide OnCreate to offer a
    "Create …" row for an unmatched query. A MudAutocomplete over OdsOption
    records, bound to the option Value via @bind-Value.

    When the record being created has a TYPE the user must choose up front — a
    contact is a person or a company — pass CreateKinds and the popover offers one
    create row per kind, each reading `Add "‹query›"` with the kind as a muted
    trailing icon + label; the picked key reaches OnCreate. The typed rows replace
    the single unqualified row, so nothing has to guess a type after the fact.

    Each option may carry a leading Material-icon ligature (OdsOption.Icon +
    IconColor): it renders before the label in every dropdown row (ItemTemplate)
    and as a leading glyph on the trigger once a value is selected (the DS
    .odc-combo-opt / .odc-input-icon language). Options without an icon render
    label-only, so icon-less pickers (e.g. tags) are unaffected.

    CoerceValue is left at its default (true) so a typed-but-unselected value
    isn't silently discarded.

    FreeText turns it into a SUGGEST-don't-constrain field: the value is a string
    the user may type freely, and the options are recognised names rather than the
    only legal answers. A value matching no option still displays, and the typed
    query commits on blur, so a name that is never explicitly picked is not
    silently dropped — the one behaviour a constrained select must NOT have. It
    switches MudAutocomplete's CoerceText / CoerceValue OFF: coercing the text to
    the (still old) value on blur would wipe the typed name, so the commit is this
    component's own, made on focus leaving the field.
*@

@inject IJSRuntime Js

@* FreeText owns both halves of its commit on this wrapper, through events Blazor dispatches
   synchronously: the typed text from the bubbled input event, and the commit from the bubbled
   focusout. MudAutocomplete updates its Text, and invokes its OnBlur, asynchronously, so relying on
   either let a fast Tab (or a slow machine) move on before the typed name was committed. *@
<div class="@WrapClass" @oninput="OnTyped" @onfocusout="CommitTyped">
    @* A real <label for> beats a splatted aria-label: MudAutocomplete renders unmatched attributes on
       its root wrapper, not the inner role="combobox" input, so an aria-label there never names the
       control. With an explicit InputId the visually-hidden label associates with the actual input. *@
    @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(AriaLabel) && !string.IsNullOrEmpty(InputId))
    {
        <label for="@InputId" class="sr-only">@AriaLabel</label>
    }
    @if (SelectedIcon is not null)
    {
        <span class="material-icons odc-combo-lead" style="@SelectedIconStyle" aria-hidden="true">@SelectedIcon</span>
    }
    <MudAutocomplete T="OdsOption"
                     Value="@Selected"
                     ValueChanged="OnSelected"
                     SearchFunc="Search"
                     ToStringFunc="@(o => o?.Label ?? string.Empty)"
                     Placeholder="@Placeholder"
                     Variant="Variant.Outlined"
                     Margin="Margin.Dense"
                     Disabled="@Disabled"
                     Clearable="@Clearable"
                     ResetValueOnEmptyText="false"
                     Class="@Class"
                     InputId="@InputId"
                     ItemDisabledFunc="@(o => o?.Disabled == true)"
                     Error="@Error"
                     CoerceText="@(!FreeText)"
                     CoerceValue="@(!FreeText)"
                     @attributes="UserAttributes">
        <ItemTemplate Context="option">
            @* aria-disabled rides on this span rather than on the row MudAutocomplete gives
               role="option" — the template cannot reach that element. MudBlazor 9.9 emits no
               aria-disabled of its own for a list item (ItemDisabledFunc blocks selection and styles
               the row), so this is not redundant, but nor is it guaranteed to be announced as the
               option's state. The meaning does not rest on it: OdsOption.Note carries the reason as
               visible, readable TEXT ("in use"), which is the design system's actual contract. *@
            <span class="@ItemClass(option)" aria-disabled="@(option.Disabled ? "true" : null)">
                @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(option.Icon))
                {
                    <span class="material-icons odc-combo-opt-ic" style="@OptionIconStyle(option)" aria-hidden="true">@option.Icon</span>
                }
                <span class="odc-combo-opt-label">@option.Label</span>
                @* A qualifier, always in WORDS — a row never says why it can't be picked by colour alone. *@
                @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(option.Note))
                {
                    <span class="odc-combo-opt-note">@option.Note</span>
                }
                @* The type the row would create — muted and trailing, never competing with the action. *@
                @if (KindOf(option) is { } kind)
                {
                    <span class="odc-combo-create-kind">
                        @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(kind.Icon))
                        {
                            <span class="material-icons" aria-hidden="true">@kind.Icon</span>
                        }
                        <span>@kind.Label</span>
                    </span>
                }
            </span>
        </ItemTemplate>
        <NoItemsTemplate>
            <div class="odc-combo-empty" role="status" aria-live="polite">@(Loading ? "Loading…" : EmptyText)</div>
        </NoItemsTemplate>
    </MudAutocomplete>
</div>
